# SYNTHETIC ARTIFACT: JWT ALG CONFUSION & EXCESSIVE DATA EXPOSURE (HAR TRACE)
**Applies to Architecture:** OmniCell 5G Provisioning Gateway v3.9.2
**Vulnerabilities Referenced:** Section 4.1 (Pattern 10.2 & Pattern 3.1)

This HTTP Archive (HAR) trace captures the moment the attacker successfully breaches the IT/OT boundary. The attacker sends a GET request to the diagnostics endpoint. 

Crucially, the `Authorization` header contains a forged JWT. If you decode the first segment of the JWT (`eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9`), it translates to `{"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}`. By forcing the algorithm to HS256, the attacker tricks the backend Node.js server into using its public RSA key string as a symmetric HMAC secret. The signature validates perfectly, granting the attacker `FIELD_TECH` authorization. 

The backend then queries the Unified Data Management (UDM) core. Because the API developer lazily returned the entire database row instead of explicitly mapping only diagnostic fields, the HTTP response body leaks the victim's `auth_key_ki` and `opc_key`—the highly restricted cryptographic master keys required to clone the physical SIM card.

